-- title:
--   Canonical finite bridge data assemble source inputs and primitive gaps
-- statement:
--   Fix a positive-width regular mesh, a finite set of primes $P$, exponent $E>0$, a source bridge with bank and guarded anchor certificate, and physical intervals contained between one and two. Let $J$ be fresh post-height bridge inputs with exponent $E$, prime cutoff supported in $P$, and canonical guarded sample geometry. Assume agreement of bank and bridge guards, sample endpoints inside the broad lower block, available rough depth, active mass $q_n\ge1$, and nonnegative protected coefficient. Suppose its rounded selector is a valid source state, and for some $C_m\ge0$, $\rho>0$,
--   $$q_n\le C_m n/\log n,\qquad |\mathrm{SampleAt}(c)|\ge\rho n\quad\text{for every cell }c.$$
--   Assume combined anchor/bank divisibility into the tail and selector-charge divisibility into the precharged target. Fix positive lower bounds for the source-cell, head, physical, and post-cell margins and suppose $J$ meets them. Finally require exact synchronization of its smooth mass, frozen mass, and final active mass with the prescribed scalar families, and the literal rounded-source residual inputs. Then there are a source-input package $S$ and a primitive-gap record for this same $J$, with
--   $$S.C_{\rm mass}=C_m,\qquad S.\mathrm{density}=\rho.$$
--   The remaining source-package properties follow from the stated canonical geometry and divisibilities.
-- source:
